The supplied interface initially left the following behavior unspecified.
The repair feedback establishes negative amounts and precedence of impossible
collection lengths over item limits. Other choices below remain assumptions
where the supplied materials do not resolve them:

- Binary fields follow conventional Bitcoin transaction serialization:
  little-endian fixed-width integers and minimally encoded CompactSize
  collection lengths. Version bits, previous indices, sequences, and locktime
  are unsigned 32-bit values; amounts are signed 64-bit values with two's-
  complement encoding. Scripts and
  witnesses are opaque bytes; no consensus, script, or monetary checks apply.
- Witness mode accepts legacy transactions and marker/flag `00 01`
  transactions. Unknown flags and superfluous all-empty witness stacks are
  malformed. A stack containing one empty item is nonempty. The `00 00`
  input/output pair represents empty vectors. Zero-input legacy transactions
  with nonempty outputs need legacy decoding because of marker ambiguity.
- Serialization emits witness marker/flag and stacks only when
  `include_witness` is true and at least one input has a nonempty stack.
  Serialization and identification validate the complete supplied object,
  including witness data when producing stripped encoding.
- Identifiers are double SHA-256 of stripped/full serialization. Digest order
  is the raw digest; display order reverses its bytes. Without witness data,
  stripped/full encodings and txid/wtxid are identical.
- Prefix offsets are byte offsets; `consumed` is the number of bytes read
  starting at that offset. Offsets beyond the supplied buffer are invalid
  requests. Insufficient bytes and exact decoding with trailing bytes are
  malformed encodings.
- `max_items` bounds each length-prefixed collection independently, including
  scripts and witness item byte lengths. It is not a cumulative budget and
  does not bound fixed-size fields or the complete transaction. Missing limits
  or an empty limits object impose no additional bound. A zero limit permits
  empty collections. Canonical length parsing and minimum byte feasibility
  checks precede the limit check. Both bounds and limits are checked before
  allocation from decoded counts. The feedback does not establish global
  precedence between a resource error and a later, unrelated encoding error.
- Required fields must have the stated JSON types; null does not substitute
  for an array, string, or limits object. Integer strings contain ASCII digits
  only, with an optional leading minus for amounts; leading zeros are accepted,
  and plus signs and whitespace are rejected. Hex is
  case-insensitive on input and lowercase on output, with no `0x` prefix.
  Unknown object fields are ignored; the last duplicate field wins.
- Invalid JSON, field types/ranges, or hex give `invalid_request`; invalid
  binary encoding gives `malformed_encoding`; explicit collection bounds give
  `resource_limit`; request allocation failure gives `execution_failure`.
  No transport or admission thresholds are specified, so the implementation
  invents none and does not emit those two statuses. Blank lines are invalid
  requests. CRLF and final lines without a newline are accepted. Responses
  are flushed individually, with request storage freed between lines.

Tests cover hand-assembled byte layouts, identifier byte order, a known
double-SHA256 digest, truncation at every byte, canonical length boundaries,
integer boundaries, malformed requests, witness flags/stacks, explicit limits,
250 deterministic randomized transactions, and executable transport behavior
including a request with a 65,536-byte script.

Repair tests compare all six feedback examples recursively, including every
response field, and cover signed minimum/maximum amounts, negative amounts,
out-of-range and malformed decimal strings, and impossible lengths for inputs,
outputs, scripts, witness stacks, and witness items. The original randomized
tests now generate amounts across the entire signed 64-bit range. All 17 Zig
tests and the executable transport tests pass after the repair.
